SOLUTIONS={
1:('DIG-01','Pair numbers that make ten: 7 + 3 = 10 and 6 + 4 = 10. The total is 20.'),
2:('SOLID-03','A square-based pyramid needs one square base and four triangular sides that meet at one apex. Net A has exactly that arrangement: each triangle folds up from one edge of the central square. The other choices contain the wrong kinds of faces.'),
3:('SEQ-02','Each fold in half doubles the number of paper layers. Starting with one layer, the counts are 2 after the first fold, 4 after the second and 8 after the third.'),
4:('DATA-03','The highest column is 8 followers and the lowest is 2. The difference is 8 − 2 = 6 students.'),
5:('ALG-03','The costs total $34 + $14 = $48. Income was only $29, so the loss was $48 − $29 = $19. A loss is costs minus income, not the total amount spent.'),
6:('SOLID-03','Keep C horizontal as the base. A, B and D fold upward along three edges of C. E folds around from D to form the fourth wall. The opening is then above C, so C is the bottom.'),
7:('DIV-04','Evaluate or use parity. The first four results are 144, 84, 324 and 44, all even. One-third of 123 is 41, which is odd. Choose E.'),
8:('DIG-03','Count zeros by place. The units digit is zero in 190, 200 and 210: three zeros. The tens digit is zero in every number from 200 to 209: ten more. There are no hundreds-place zeros. Total: 3 + 10 = 13. The number 200 correctly contributes two zeros.'),
9:('SPATIAL-03','Fit the six-square L along the top and right edges of the 4-by-4 grid. Put the five-square bent piece in the upper-left remainder: its cells can be row 1 column 1; row 2 columns 1,2,3; and row 3 column 3. The five cells left over form a 2-by-2 block with one extra cell attached along one end. That is shape B. Counting five remaining squares alone is not enough; their arrangement must fit.'),
10:('FRAC-02','Compared with 1001/77, the numerator 100.1 is divided by 10 and the denominator 770 is multiplied by 10. The quotient is therefore divided by 100. Its value is 13 ÷ 100 = 0.13.'),
11:('GEO-01','The overlap is a square of area 16 cm², so its side is 4 cm. The two separate perimeters total 80 cm. Four 4 cm edge segments lie inside the combined shape and no longer count as outside boundary. Subtract 16 cm: the perimeter is 64 cm.'),
12:('FRAC-01','Three-quarters of the pizza remains. Shared among six boys, each share is (3/4) ÷ 6 = 3/24 = 1/8 of the original whole pizza.'),
13:('GEO-02','Count whole grid squares and pair half-squares. Region A contains 11½ grid-square units: its four horizontal strips contribute 1½, 3, 4 and 3. Each other region occupies a three-by-four box but has enough missing corner pieces to be no more than 10½ units. Therefore A has the greatest shaded area.'),
14:('GEO-04','Undo each fold by reflecting the pinhole across that fold line. This creates eight holes, one in each 10-by-10 square, with neighbouring columns and the two rows mirrored. Only A has the required reflections across every fold. Repeating the hole at the same corner in every small square would ignore the reversal caused by folding.'),
15:('ALG-04','Undo the steps in reverse order: 2 + 2 = 4; then 4 × 2 = 8; then 8 − 2 = 6; then 6 ÷ 2 = 3. Check forward: 3 → 6 → 8 → 4 → 2.'),
16:('FRAC-02','There are 2 + 5 + 1 = 8 parts in all. Two yellow parts use 3 litres, so one part is 1.5 litres. The full batch is 8 × 1.5 = 12 litres.'),
17:('GEO-02','Each folded corner is a right triangle with legs 3 cm and 7 cm, so its area is ½ × 3 × 7 = 10.5 cm². Four corners remove 42 cm² from the original 100 cm² outline, leaving a 58 cm² tilted square. Those same four triangles now lie inside that outline, covering 42 cm². The uncovered internal square therefore has area 58 − 42 = 16 cm².'),
18:('FRAC-01','Use sixteenths as a common denominator. In B, 1/2 + 1/8 + 1/16 = 8/16 + 2/16 + 1/16 = 11/16. That is the requested fraction.'),
19:('GEO-02','Split the central regular hexagon into six equal triangles. Together with the six outer tips, the star contains twelve equal small triangles. The shaded original triangle contains the central six plus three tips: nine of the twelve. Its area is 9/12 × 60 = 45 cm².'),
20:('DIG-05','Adding two two-digit numbers gives a three-digit result, so S = 1. In the tens column, 5 + Q plus a carry must give 14. The only possible choice is a carry of 1 and Q = 8. Thus P + R = 13. Because all digits differ from 1,3,4,5,8 and from each other, P and R are 6 and 7 in either order. Their total with Q and S is 13 + 8 + 1 = 22.'),
21:('ALG-03','Let Brian’s score be b. Then Sally has 2b, Corrie has 2b − 30, and Donna has b + 50. Sally plus Donna score 3b + 50, while Brian plus Corrie score 3b − 30. The former total is always 80 points greater, whatever b is. Thus E is definitely true; the individual rankings can change.'),
22:('DATA-03','The graph must start with a horizontal segment for 20 minutes. The climb adds 5 × 500 = 2500 m, reaching 7000 m. The descent removes 8 × 350 = 2800 m, ending at 4200 m, slightly below the initial 4500 m. The descent lasts longer but is less steep than the climb. Graph E matches these features.'),
23:('RATE-02','The backyard hose fills 1/12 of the pool per hour and the front hose fills 1/4. Together they fill 1/12 + 3/12 = 1/3 per hour. One whole pool therefore takes 3 hours.'),
24:('COUNT-05','Four teams make six different pairs, and each pair plays four times: 24 games. A decisive game awards 3 points in total; a draw awards only 2. If all games were decisive, the points would total 24 × 3 = 72. The actual total is 21 + 17 + 16 + 12 = 66. Each draw accounts for a deficit of one point, so there were 6 draws.'),
25:('GEO-05','To make the largest chosen number as large as possible, make the other four different positive numbers as small as possible: 1, 2, 3 and 4. Their sum is 10, leaving 30 − 10 = 20 for the largest. The set 1,2,3,4,20 meets every condition, so 20 is achievable and maximal.')}
